186045417] [17592186485496 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{27}}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485496 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f188433-9891-58cb-8279-ce3757720a45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485496 #edb/ref 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{27}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{27}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{27}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{27}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{27}}{\\sqrt{27}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{27}}{(\\sqrt{27})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{27}}{27} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that $27$ has a perfect square factor $(9).$ Therefore, we can rewrite $\\sqrt{27}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{27} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9\\cdot 3} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify our original fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{27}}{27} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}}{27} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{27} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{9} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5\\cdot 3}}{9} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485520 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485520 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485520 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485520 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e56e0ed-1ba2-5a25-8811-643841869c5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485520 #edb/ref 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{6}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{6}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{